1
00:00:00,267 --> 00:00:11,761
This video shows how to put an animation from Pixodesk SVG Animator on a plain web page. It takes one script and one element, with no build step.

2
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:15,299
Let's start with the page as it is.

3
00:00:19,433 --> 00:00:21,837
A heading, and nothing that moves yet.

4
00:00:23,067 --> 00:00:28,761
To play animations, the page needs the Pixodesk player. Let's copy it into the project.

5
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:38,882
One command copied the player script into the folder.

6
00:00:40,100 --> 00:00:45,481
Now for the animation itself. Let's switch to the editor, where a football is already drawn.

7
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:49,743
It's a vector drawing, standing still.

8
00:00:50,967 --> 00:00:53,631
To make it move, let's switch on Auto mode.

9
00:00:56,133 --> 00:00:59,581
With Auto mode on, every change becomes a keyframe.

10
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:04,300
Half a second in, let's drag the ball down to the floor.

11
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:10,900
A keyframe appears on the timeline, holding the new position.

12
00:01:12,133 --> 00:01:13,884
At one second, let's bring it back up.

13
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:20,491
Now it falls and rises again, in one second.

14
00:01:21,733 --> 00:01:28,238
A real ball speeds up as it falls, and slows down as it rises. Let's set that easing.

15
00:01:35,367 --> 00:01:39,546
Gravity easing, falling and rising, makes the bounce feel natural.

16
00:01:40,767 --> 00:01:44,058
One bounce is not enough, so let's loop the animation.

17
00:01:47,533 --> 00:01:51,687
Loop is saved with the file, so the animation repeats forever.

18
00:01:53,900 --> 00:01:58,733
The animation is ready. Let's save it as JSON, next to the page.

19
00:02:03,700 --> 00:02:05,999
Now, back to our page.

20
00:02:07,533 --> 00:02:11,034
The animation file is already there, next to the page.

21
00:02:12,267 --> 00:02:15,062
Let's load the player, at the end of the page.

22
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:28,798
The script tag loads the player, and one call finds every animation on the page.

23
00:02:30,033 --> 00:02:33,586
Then, let's add an element that names the animation file.

24
00:02:43,133 --> 00:02:50,578
Its data attribute points at the file. The player plays it as the file says, starting on load and repeating forever.

25
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:54,464
Let's reload the page, and watch.

26
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:03,492
There it is. The ball bounces, on a plain web page.

27
00:03:05,533 --> 00:03:15,225
So, one copied script, one file saved from the editor, and one element. That's all it takes to bring an animation onto a web page.
